WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.751
Script control is one of the most
important parts of CSP because script execution is

2
00:00:07.831 --> 00:00:12.493
often the high-impact outcome of browser
injection.

3
00:00:12.573 --> 00:00:19.928
Inline scripts are risky because injected
markup may try to create script execution

4
00:00:20.008 --> 00:00:21.872
directly in the page.

5
00:00:21.952 --> 00:00:29.411
A policy that allows unsafe-inline weakens
CSP protection because it permits inline

6
00:00:29.491 --> 00:00:32.288
script execution more broadly.

7
00:00:32.368 --> 00:00:39.308
A mature policy usually works toward
reducing or eliminating dangerous script

8
00:00:39.388 --> 00:00:45.189
allowances rather than adding them
permanently for convenience.

9
00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:51.692
Nonces and hashes provide ways to allow
specific trusted inline scripts.

10
00:00:51.772 --> 00:00:57.158
A nonce is a unique value generated for a
particular response.

11
00:00:57.238 --> 00:01:04.179
The server places that value in the policy
and on the trusted script elements for

12
00:01:04.259 --> 00:01:05.606
that response.

13
00:01:05.686 --> 00:01:12.523
A hash allows specific inline script or
style content that matches an expected

14
00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:14.467
cryptographic hash.

15
00:01:14.547 --> 00:01:19.002
Both approaches require careful
implementation.

16
00:01:19.082 --> 00:01:23.743
Nonces must be unpredictable and fresh per
response.

17
00:01:23.823 --> 00:01:30.453
Hashes must match the exact trusted
content and be updated when that content

18
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:31.362
changes.

19
00:01:31.442 --> 00:01:37.657
unsafe-eval and broad script source
allowlists also deserve caution.

20
00:01:37.737 --> 00:01:44.263
Some frameworks or build settings may rely
on evaluation-like behavior in

21
00:01:44.343 --> 00:01:47.451
development but not in production.

22
00:01:47.531 --> 00:01:50.431
strict-dynamic, at a high level,

23
00:01:50.511 --> 00:01:58.177
can let trusted nonce-bearing scripts load
additional scripts in supported browsers,

24
00:01:58.257 --> 00:02:02.608
changing how source allowlists are
interpreted.

25
00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:07.556
Teams should use well-documented framework
guidance,

26
00:02:07.636 --> 00:02:11.780
reduce dangerous script allowances over
time,

27
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:19.008
and treat script policy as an architecture
decision rather than a header pasted in

28
00:02:19.088 --> 00:02:20.020
at the end.
